title: Syncope And Assimilation
---

The two phenomena detailed below, syncope and assimilation, exist in Icelandic to make the words easier to pronounce. The exact rules governing these phenomena are not important, but it is important that you can recognize them when they occur. 

## Syncope
In Nordic languages, including Icelandic, syncope refers to the omission of one or more sounds or syllables from a word, often to simplify pronunciation. As a general rule, Nordic languages don't like for words to contain more than two syllables unless they are compounds, so when words become three syllables because of endings being added, they will often omit non-stress syllables to reduce the word to two syllables. 

Example: Gamalar -> Gamlar

In the lesson Adjectives, more examples of this sort will be provided.  

## Assimilation
Assimilation is the changing of consonants to be more similar to each other to simplify pronunciation. 

Example: Hraðt -> Hratt

The assimilation above happened because *-ðt* would be a very hard consonant cluster to pronounce, so the ending is changed to *-tt** to make it easier. 

